  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on the skewness of the lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more left-skewed (i.e., assigns higher probability to favorable outcomes relative to unfavorable ones). To evaluate this, we must determine the skewness direction of each lottery. In Lottery A: there is a 90% chance of losing 3000 Euros (unfavorable) and 10% chance of 0 (neutral/favorable). Thus, most probability mass is on an unfavorable outcome, making it left-skewed only if 0 is considered favorable enough to count as a 'favorable tail'—but since the dominant outcome is a loss, it is actually right-skewed (long tail on the left, but probability mass on the worse outcome). In Lottery B: 45% chance of losing 6000 (unfavorable), 55% chance of 0 (favorable). Here, the favorable outcome (0) has higher probability than the unfavorable one, so this is left-skewed. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery B because it is more left-skewed (higher probability on the better outcome). The information needed—probabilities and outcomes—is available, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The individual considers the probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros) in both lotteries. They note that in Lottery B, the chance of escaping unscathed is over 50%, making it more likely than incurring a loss. In contrast, Lottery A involves relatively certain (high probability) losses, even if smaller. The individual frames the choice in terms of likelihood of avoiding loss versus facing a loss, emphasizing the higher safety (zero outcome) probability in B. Their justification centers on the comparative likelihood of escaping loss, which they view as more favorable in B. They do not mention skewness explicitly, but they do focus on the probability distribution of outcomes, particularly the chance of the best possible result (0 Euros).",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing which lottery assigns more probability to favorable outcomes (left-skewness). The individual considers exactly this: the probability of achieving the best outcome (0 Euros) versus a loss. They recognize that B has a higher chance (55%) of the favorable outcome compared to A (10%), which aligns with the skewness-based evaluation. Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the cognitive process—comparing the likelihood of favorable outcomes and favoring the one with higher probability on the better outcome—matches the rule of the decision reason. The individual’s focus is not on expected value, variance, or loss magnitude, but on the probability of avoiding loss, which is the core of left-skewness in this context. Therefore, the information considered and the justification align closely with the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
